WEBVTT

00:03.899 --> 00:05.166
In this work,

00:05.166 --> 00:07.533
We proposed a vision-based decentralized relative

00:07.533 --> 00:10.933
 pose-estimation architecture based on GNNs.

00:11.599 --> 00:15.500
 Our key idea is the implicit virtual leader (IVL),

00:15.533 --> 00:17.966
 a non-physical formation reference frame implicitly

00:17.966 --> 00:21.066
 learned by the neural network from monocular images

00:21.066 --> 00:23.100
 and inter robot communication.

00:24.933 --> 00:26.733
Relying solely on visual input,

00:26.733 --> 00:29.966
 our network achieves relative-pose based formation control

00:29.966 --> 00:33.100
 without the need for any additional localization sensors.

00:35.166 --> 00:36.766
In our network architecture,

00:36.799 --> 00:40.333
 all robots operate as equal peers without a centralized hierarchy

00:40.433 --> 00:43.066
powered by decentralized edge inference.

00:44.799 --> 00:48.766
This enables zero latency, dynamic switching of the leader at any time.

00:51.000 --> 00:53.299
If any robot goes offline,

00:53.299 --> 00:56.233
 the remaining peer seamlessly maintain the formation.

00:57.000 --> 00:59.700
This eliminates the single-point-of-failure inherent

00:59.700 --> 01:01.700
in traditional leader-follower modes,

01:01.700 --> 01:04.000
 significantly boosting system reliability.

01:13.500 --> 01:14.733
Trained just once,

01:14.733 --> 01:17.400
our network can deployed across heterogeneous robots,

01:17.400 --> 01:19.233
 such as wheeled and quadruped robots,

01:19.233 --> 01:21.633
enabling zero-shot deployment.

01:30.266 --> 01:32.500
Even in the absence of visual overlap,

01:32.500 --> 01:36.466
our architecture maintains highly accurate relative pose estimation.

01:40.966 --> 01:42.799
When encountering obstacles,

01:42.799 --> 01:48.633
our network outputs reliable uncertainty estimation to guarantee formation safety.

01:49.599 --> 01:51.400
In this experiment,

01:51.400 --> 01:54.099
The blue RoboMaster automatically stops due to high uncertainty，

01:54.099 --> 01:57.066
while the other two adapt into a two-robot formation.

02:02.833 --> 02:05.366
Once the obstacle is removed,

02:05.366 --> 02:09.300
the re-online blue robot automatically resumes the collaborative formation.

02:15.500 --> 02:18.199
To validate the pose estimation accuracy，

02:18.199 --> 02:23.199
we demonstrate heterogeneous multi-robot formation switching through a narrow tunnel.

02:24.266 --> 02:28.966
With a tunnel width of only about 3 RoboMaster widths (0.72m),

02:29.400 --> 02:33.066
our method delivers a success rate close to 100%.

02:34.633 --> 02:39.199
Lastly, we demonstrate a 5-robot formation with low latency.

02:40.966 --> 02:44.099
In the future, we plan to extend this framework to achieve

02:44.099 --> 02:47.400
more complex multi-robot obstacle avoidance, planning and navigation,

02:47.400 --> 02:50.266
empowering multi-robot systems with ultimate resilience

02:50.266 --> 02:52.800
and seamless, cross-platform adaptability.
